      "claim": "The automatic re-queue can never fire in production. Without --again, `work land` re-queues a returned tip by itself only when returnedPathsResolved is true. That check requires three things at once: the rebase's old tip, the rebase's new tip and the returned commit are all the same commit, AND every path the return names is listed in the rebase's Regenerated or Resolved list. Real code fills Regenerated only when the rebase moved the branch to a new commit, and nothing ever fills Resolved. So the check is always false outside tests. The subtests 'regenerated' and 'resolved' pass only because they build rebase results that cannot occur. The design's clause 'work land sets it itself when the return names only paths the rebase resolved and the branch is unchanged' therefore does nothing in practice. Not material under R-124 (Wido's rule that a finding counts only when the slice does not work or is not safe without the fix): the explicit --again path works and is safe, a branch the rebase changed is a new commit that gets a fresh waiting line anyway, and a path that never fires cannot re-queue anything wrongly. It belongs on the design's 'later, when it hurts' list, or the design clause should be corrected.",
